  "Number": 26,
  "Question": "The number of distinct positive integral divisors of \\((30)^4\\) excluding \\(1\\) and \\((30)^4\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(100\\)",
    "B": "\\(125\\)",
    "C": "\\(123\\)",
    "D": "\\(30\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Prime factorization makes the divisor choices explicit:\n\\[\n30^4=(2\\cdot3\\cdot5)^4=2^4 3^4 5^4.\n\\]\nEvery positive divisor has the form \\(2^i3^j5^k\\), where each of \\(i,j,k\\) is one of \\(0,1,2,3,4\\). Every such choice gives a divisor, and unique prime factorization ensures that different exponent triples give different divisors. Thus the count is\n\\[\n5\\cdot5\\cdot5=125.\n\\]\nThe triple \\((0,0,0)\\) gives \\(1\\), and \\((4,4,4)\\) gives \\(30^4\\). Removing these two distinct divisors leaves\n\\[\n125-2=123.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
